speechtext:
I had in mind two different things at that time. So far as the policy of protection is concerned, I say there was no new policy introduced in 1897, 1898 or 1900. If you wish to deal with the British preference as affecting protection, that is another question. The British preference as brought in by the Liberal party in 1897 was a new policy.
To sum up what may be characterized as the spirit of the Minister of Finance's speech, I may say that he has indicated plainly to this House, by all the figures he has presented, that Canada of to-day is in a prosperous condition. Canada being healthy, the fewer changes that are made in the conditions that contribute to that health, the better it will be. I do not think that the Minister of Finance, from what I can judge, of his utterances, has any intention or desire to make radical changes in the existing system. I think he is wise. He heeds the warning given cn a tombstone in an English churchyard:
I	was well;
I	wanted to be better;
And here I lie.
Canada to-day is progressing along safe, broad, prosperous lines, and it would be unwise for us to do any experimenting which might tend to less prosperity than we enjoy to-day.
